  "description": "Sittuyin, Burmese chess. The pawns start on a staggered line across the third and fourth ranks; every other piece starts in hand, and while a player still holds one, a move is placing it on an empty square of their own three back ranks, a chariot (yahhta) on the back rank only. Then the game is makruk's pieces: a pawn on its long diagonal of promotion squares may become a general, but only once its side's general has been lost. Stalemate loses for the side that cannot move.",
